Date: Mon, 17 Jan 94 22:18:16 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: BOT: How do trees drink

First: congratulations to Amara and Vince.  Sadly I shall have to 
physically absent myself from the happy occasion.

Second: today I checked up about xylem transport of sap in _Plant 
Physiology_ by L Taiz and E Zeiger (1991).

Capillary action/adhesion to the xylem walls can not explain the lifting
of sap over a 100m or so (as in Redwoods).  According to their figures
capillary action (pgs 64-66) would only explain a rise of a couple of
centimetres in a typical xylem tube.

The tensile strength of pure water has been experimentally measured (in
"narrow" tubes) as about -300 atmospheres.  The maximum theoretical
value is estimated at about -1000 atmospheres.  In water with dissolved
gases the tensile strength is reduced by cavitation (which is when the
gases form bubbles and explode).  Sap is very low on dissolved gases.

The accepted theory is that there is a negative pressure gradient
driving the flow of sap (at about 1-50 metres/hour, depending on xylem
tube radii).  The main drag on sap flow is viscosity rather than gravity
with a Redwood exerting a pull of about -30 atmospheres: -10 on
overcoming gravity and -20 on combating viscosity.  So a Redwood could
grow to a kilometre in height and still suck water up from its roots.

Cavitation does occur in the xylem tubes.  Under the tensions imposed
bubbles explode in the sap (this noise can be detected with sensitive
microphones against) and "take out" a section of tubing.  Sap has to
bypass such sections until the section is reflooded.  This happens at
night when transpiration from the leaves stop, internal pressures return
to positive and the gases redissolve.

