Date: Mon, 17 Jan 1994 22:20:54 -0800
From: vincek@netcom.com (Vince Kerchner)
Subject: Net tech

In my opinion, current networking software --including that which I'm
using to write to you now-- really sucks.  It's slow, has an
antiquated command line interface with lots of little minus options,
and passes everything through a text-based bottleneck.

On the other hand, I really like my Mac.  It's reasonably zippy, has a
great graphical interface that lets me manipulate and classify files
visually, and lets me manage text, graphics, movies, and sound in
pseudo-real time.

It seems to me that we ought to be able to combine the two.
Specifically, we ought to be able to run AppleShare over internet, and
thereby do all our networking operations as if they were being
performed on the Mac desktop.  Given current hardware technology, I
believe that sufficient bandwidth exists to transparently support this
paradigm for all internet operations.  

For example, my present modem operates at 14.4 Kbs, which is more than
half the local AppleTalk rate of 23 Kbs.  If the software is written
correctly, and appropriate compression technology is used, adequate
continuous subjective user response should be achievable.  Given that
current software using the same physical channel provides absolutely
horrible user response, such an experience would be no less than
revolutionary. 

Yes, I know about things like Eudora and Mosiac.  However, these are
stand-alone systems which are specifically tailored for focused
subsets of the above.  And besides that, they aren't all that great
yet.  What I'm looking for is something that seemlessly integrates
with the rest of the Mac OS and can be used in *all* phases of normal
day-to-day computation.

Let me give you an example.  Let's say I turn on my computer and want
to check the activity on the List.  First, I go to the Chooser and
select AppleShare.  One of my options would be "Netcom Internet
Server", currently offline.  As I select this, my computer and modem
dial up netcom and log me in, and a Netcom icon appears on my desktop.
If I check the appropriate box, this activity can be performed
automatically whenever I boot the machine.

If I double-click on this icon, I get a folder of services which
appear exactly as if they were normal Mac directories (just as if I
had logged into another Mac down the hall).  One of these might be
"Mailing Lists", which might contain "Extropy List" and "Exi Bay List"
as sub-folders.  Opening "Extropy List", I see a directory of folders
which represent the current threads of ongoing discussions.  This
would be similar to the digest form of the List to which many
subscribe.  However, it would be a real-time digest, and would get
updated whenever someone made a change to the thread, not only after
intervals of time or content size.

Looking in the folder of a particular thread, I see the individual
posts as normal Mac files.  In fact, to me these *are* normal Mac
files, and can be of any format and contain any data.  To read a post,
I can either double-click on it in the thread folder, or drag it to my
own system (which copies the file) and open it there.  If I wish to
make a post, I can either create a file on my own system and drag it
into the thread folder, or save it directly to the thread folder from
whatever program I use to create it.  

If I want to create a new thread, I just do a "New Folder" from the
Finder when the "Extropy List" window is active (hilighted), select
the new icon, and type in the name.  I can then drag or save files to
this folder, and they appear as posts to everyone else logged in.
Others may also post to this new thread, again simply by dragging or
saving files to this folder.  This would eliminate the current
multi-thread problem that occurs when different spellings are used in 
the subject line of a post.

Through this process, I can upload or download any data or file type,
not just text.  Nor do I have to go through the pain of binhexing
non-text data.  I can create aliases, and I can run programs (although
due to speed limitations I'd probably want to drag them to my own disk
first).  I can apply read/write permissions to my public files and
folders, and can create users and groups for controlled access.  What
I'm describing may sound like AppleLink, but it's really much more.
It is seemless integration of AppleShare into world-wide internet
services. 

How might we get this to work without first being forced to
fundamentally change the structure and operation of all current
internet hosts and services?  That's the big question, but I think it
could be done.  On the Mac side, it would be "relatively" easy.  We'd
need to write a Finder extension which would manage the Chooser
selection of and connection to the local internet server.  That should
be pretty straight-forward.  Next, we'd need to write a driver which
would access the modem and mount it as a normal AppleShare disk.
Again, that should be relatively straight-forward (although there's
quite a lot to do).  In fact, it might be possible to modify ARA
(AppleTalk Remote Access, a current Apple product) to do both of the
above. 

The Unix side would be a little harder.  We'd need to write a new
program which could be launched over the normal tty device driver to
interface with our Mac driver.  It would have to translate between
Unix directory format (by reading I-nodes) and directories and files
on the Mac.  In essence, it would manage the datastream necessary to
represent the Unix directory tree and permission system as a graphical
user interface on the Mac, and vice-versa.  It would also have to
manage the interface and translation between the multiple creator and
file types, icon and other Finder info, and resource fork, of Mac
files, and the text or binary file representation of the server.  In
addition, both the Mac and Unix drivers may have to transparently
manage the binhexing/unbinhexing of binary data from the Mac to the
server when the tty driver is in ASCII mode.

In addition to making the whole internet process more enjoyable and
efficient, this procedure could also promote the royalty-based
distribution of published material, similar to that proposed by Amix.
This feature could be implemented as a direct extension of the
"enclosure" process currently used by AppleLink.  Whenever a file is
posted to a public folder on a server, it could be maintained in two
different forms: one which is freely available to other users, and one
which requires the authorization of a royalty fee to be paid to the
author before downloading can occur.  The secure file could be
visible to the user, but unopenable/uncopyable through the use of
AppleShare permissions.

A "free" file would behave just like a current internet or AppleLink
memo, and could contain either the target data itself, or a link to a
secure "enclosure" file and a description of the contents of that
file.  In essence, this would be an advertisement.  Users who wish to
download the attached enclosure must acknowledge the transfer of a
"fee" (real or otherwise) which is automatically made to the author's
account as part of the downloading process.  This process could be
managed by a simple button or menu item which is enabled whenever a
document with a royalty-based enclosure is open and active.  Depending
on the security of the Mac-based front end software, it may even be
possible to "return" downloaded (but not saved) royalty files if the
contents are unacceptable, and thereby void the transaction.

Some of you might ask:  "Why would I want to do this if it only works
on a Mac?"  Well, if this system can provide all that I think it can,
and if Apple can successfully market a machine which can provide the
necessary hardware horsepower for about $1000 (it can), then maybe you
*should* go out and buy a Mac as a dedicated internet front end.  That's
what free market economics are all about, right?

--Vince

