blurring of boundaries, as in revelations of personal confidences
(whether or not a "contract" exists, which is often the only way
libertarians look at things), statements of anger about personal
differences on a publically read list, weeping, and so on.

An ex-GF of mine was an MFCC, a licensed "Marriage, Family, and Child
Counselor." That is, she dealt with family therapy, suicide
counseling, sexual issues, and the like. Definitely not a hard-core
Libertarian (though a libertine!), she taught me this usage. And it
explains a lot. 

Though I despise certain nonthinking, knee jerk uses of psychobabble,
this is a useful idea. Examples: where people draw boundaries, ranging
from the well-known national differences in physical proximity (e.g.,
Americans like to stand about 2.5-3 feet apart when talking, Italians
typically move in to less than 1.5 feet), to differences between men
and women in touching (women like to touch and be touched, except they
often scream "harassment!" when men misjudge...and many men dislike
being touched, except in sexual situations...just some examples--your
mileage may vary).

"Poor boundaries" can also result in assumptions about what other
people have or have not volunteered to do, as in the recent flap (on
Exi-Bay) about communal shooting parties, and in posting party
invitations inappropriately. I'm not saying this is a mortal sin, just
that "boundary problems" show up all the time in human interactions.

My chief complain vis-a-vis this list is that some members have gotten
"overly familiar," discussing their reactions to other people in a way
that has no place on a list like this. (Something I may be doing here,
of course, but this is the usual result of boundaries--some would call
it "common decency" or just "manners"--being transgressed.)


> >It's not an extended family ...
> >
> After hearing how unclose many of us are with our families, maybe we should
> be glad it's not an extended family!

Precisely! The current issues, where one person is "sharing" his/her
highly personal feelings about another (me, if you can't guess) are
precisely the dynamics that happen in dysfunctional families! Yes,
this is my heresy...the normal interpretation, a la Bradshaw, is that
dysfunctional families arise because of failure of communication.
Bottled up emotions, etc. No doubt this is sometimes true.

But problems just as often originate in overly-personal revelations
about highly personal opinions. Contrary to the notion that "problems"
in lists or in families are "worked out" by extremely open
communication, the opposite is often the case. What's putatively
therapeutic for the complainer is often just the opening salvo of a
multi-year process of sobbing confessions, group therapy, and eventual
exhaustion.

In any case, whether is eventually helps the complainer is beside the
point on a list like this: because we are not that person's "group"
and we have little interest in hearing these personal psychodramas.

(I'm speaking for myself. If enough others disagree with me, the
Extropians list can of course be turned into a recovery group. I'm not
here for that, buy you folks are welcome to make the list what you
want it to be.)

In the context of this list, I have dislikes for many of the personality
characteristics of many on this list. Are things made better if I
"share" these views with you?

The joke "Thank you for sharing that with us" is common not only
amongst laypersons like ourselves, but also amongst therapists I know.
Because they realize that much of modern group therapy is dealing with
people who can't recognize proper ego boundaries and who insist,
destructively, on "sharing" their deepest emotional demons and fears.
It's become a cliche in the therapy business.

I've said it several times before and I'll say it again: some list
members apparently see this list as a new kind social group therapy
session, to do a "show and tell" in their progress toward "Recovery"
(from whatever haunts them...drugs, Objectivism, oppressive parents,
Lifespring, est, unhappy childhoods, religion,  etc.).

Not what I think the list is about.

Kathy goes on to say:

> If all we can come up with is that an "Extropian relationship" is one
> which is in accord with Extropian Principles, we haven't accomplished
> anything interesting! It seems that there is a lot of definition-hunting

I agree, and the list is becoming more boring these last few months
for it. I gather that the vast majority of list members do not have
partners or family who are subscribed to the list (the examples I am
aware of are two couples in the Bay Area, Amara/Vince (a newcomer) and
Romana/Geoff)...I am not saying that folks on this list hang out with
anti-Extropian "Entropians," only that in most cases only one part of
the partnership is interested enough to subscribe and participate.

I don't recall the specifics, but there was something in Geoff Dale's
piece about accepting the transformations of one's partner(s). This
struck me as empty of content. If the transformations leave the person
even more sexually and emotionally attractive to one, this is
fine. But if not, what's "self-transformation" got to do with it? To
me, it's all personal taste, mutually of course, and "Extropian
Principles" seem pretty superfluous

If a partner of mine decided to pierce her nose or any other body
part, it'd be "Sayonara!" for her. It has to do with personal taste,
my taste. And ditto for her. "Extropianism" has little to do with
these matters of personal aesthetics, of the process of attraction,
just as "Marxism" or "libertarianism" has little to do with it.
(Remember the crazy Marxists who promised each other "Marxist
marriages"?).

I'm equally unconvinced that ohter "Extropian Principles" can
reasonably be extended into the domain of interpersonal relationships,
except as general libertarian outlooks. I'm happy for Amara and Vince,
and Romana and Geoff, and for any other couples, but I'm not especially
persuaded that some ideas Max More wrote about, stuff about "Silicon
Transubstantiation" and "Primeval Revival" or whatever the
"Principles" really are, tell us much about finding and maintaining
stable relationships.

(Yes, I've read the infamous "Issue #3," or whatever issue it is, as I
bought all the back issues from Max when I visited him in L.A. a year
ago. I respect what Max and Tom created, but I have no illusions that
they discovered some set of Cosmic Principles as they were putting
together their early issues. Frankly, the traffic on this list has
probably had 50 times as many megabytes of thinking--some flaky, some
mundane, some sublime--as in all of the first dozen or so issues of
"Extropy.")

> I strive to attain a high level of autonomy in which I admit that some
> decions are tough, decide freely anyway, and accept full responsibility for
> these decisions. I never want to be an extropian zealot or ideologue who
> makes decisions based on the Extropian Holy Book. No thanks - I'll trust my
> own godshatter!

Right on! The whole shtick about Extropian Principles seems misguided.

> I find myself most at peace when I drop such categorizations and realize
> that the interesting problems are in the fuzzy boundaries. (Back to that!)
> The more ideological baggage I can shed, the better I feel. I come to this
> list for discussion of interesting topics, not to find new blinders.

Again, I agree. I know Geoff has, in a later message, asked that
discussions of "Is Extropianism becoming a religion?" be taken to
private mail, but this issue will not go away so easily.

Especially given that some of the folks putting together a FAQ on
Extropianism are also centrally involved in this discussion of
relationships, epistemology, etc. I am happy that _some_ group is
addressing the longstanding FAQ problem, but Geoff cannot raise the
issue of "Extropian Relationships" in a long (and welcome, despite my
disagreements with his conclusions) essay and then, when folks raise
concerns about views being adopted as a new Extropian catechism,
suggest they are not appropriate topics for the list as a whole to
discuss.

I think there's a very real danger that this list is evolving into a
kind of support group/"Lifespring"-type movement that is, in many
ways, equivalent to a religion. While the trappings of God and
prophets are seemingly gone, in fact there is a set of Holy Books--the
back issues of "Extropy" (which seldom get discussed here,
ironically), the same appeals to authority ("Principle Five says..."),
and  the same pseudo-religious cant about the nature of the Universe
and Man's place in it.

(I'm aware of the differences with conventional religions, too, having
spent a lot of time last spring and summer on the "Paganism" issue.
The Promethean vs. Gaian dichotomy remains something I hope to someday
report on to the list.)

I can't think of a topic more worthy of discussion and less worthy of
casual dismissal as having already been discussed and resolved.

--Tim May


