>
In my vanity, I assume that Vince is referring to my recent post on the
"Extropian Love" thread, in which I said:
** begin quote **
I think it might be useful to consider such relationships.  I'd say the nun
is doing the reasonable thing based on her heartfelt convictions and her
assessments of the consequences of her actions, however she might have come
by those convictions and assessments.  The relationship is self-determined
at least insofar as the concept of "personal relationship with God" is
present and the nun has the freedom to leave the convent.

In this as well as other areas of this discussion, it might be useful to
include rather than exclude, and if we can't find a category for something,
make a new category, rather than tossing the whole thing out.  As my friend
Jim says:  "A pile for everything, and everything in its pile!".
** end quote **

Don't panic.  I am not expressing a religious viewpoint here.  I am
discussing religion as an emotional and psychological phenomenon.  I think
that all of us have relationships with our interpretations of a person, not
with the person him/herself.  The extent to which these interpretations
coincide with the Schelling point of the interpretations that others have
of that person, partly (mainly?) based on their power to predict the
person's future behavior, is how we judge the reasonableness of that
interpretation -- for example, the woman who says that the jailed
axe-murderer she is about to marry "really is a good man" can be said to
have gone far afield of that point.

A nun having a relationship with God can be said to be an extreme example
of this, and the psychology and emotionality of that relationship is worth
studying as a data point.  For example (one of many):  many nuns clearly
think they are getting more out of their relationship with God than just a
promise of a hereafter -- what do they think they're getting, and (assuming
that the nuns are not all simply insane) to what extent can people in
terrestrial relationships be said to be getting similar things from
similarly insubstantial sources?

The relationships that some people insane in various ways have with their
loved ones, the relationships of some clients to their therapists, the
relationships of some people in distress to their rescuers (or to their
torturers), etc., are all data points in love-relationship space, and
reflection on how the ideal Extropian relationship might be like, and how
it might be unlike, each of these can help us gain insight on the
phenomenon known as "Extropian Love".

BTW:  not believing in God (or Shiva or Aphrodite or Quetzalcoatl or The
Bird Spirit or Oshun) doesn't mean I have to vilify or insult people who
do.  I just want them not to ram their gods down my throat or use them to
intimidate or coerce me.

In tolerance and inquiry,
Kennita


