Subject: Claim: Extropians *Is* Becoming a Religion, of Sorts

   Recently, a friend of mine (Shawn, smo@gnu) was reviewing Max
Sterner's book for me and one of Sterner's ideas is very applicable to
this "Extropian Religion" business. We're selfish, individualistic,
people, right? If that's so, we shouldn't serve an IDEA, we should
make the idea serve us. In other words, don't do something just for
the sake of an ideal, do it because it pleases YOU. A lot of
Objectivists are unhappy because they can't reconcile each and every
desire/action with their philsophy. That's serving an ideal. If an
altruistic relationship pleases you, DO IT, if it doesn't, DON'T DO
IT. Don't draw up legalistic/philosophical arguments to rationalize
your relationship based on a higher ideal above yourself.  You will
only succeed in hurting yourself when you don't live up to these
ideals.

   The extropian principles are a guideline, not a set of commandments.
Don't adhere to them if it hurts you. Personally, I use them only as
a mechanism to filter noise and perhaps a roadmap for how I would like
to move towards in the future. I'm interested in the ideas in the 
extropian principles but they are not rules which, if I don't obey them,
make me an immoral or unlikable person.


  I was talking with Shawn about Sterner and how he kind of boiled
down all these philosophies by creating an isomorphism between them. Then
by criticizing the base case, he commented on all of them. The problem
with each and every one of them was that people served an idea for
the sake of the idea and not for themselves. The moral: do what you
want and be happy.


  I plan on reading Sterner's book soon.

-Ray
 
  

